\section{Complexity and performance analysis}
We analyze token-level and asymptotic costs of core algorithms and discuss practical considerations for latency/compute trade-offs.

\subsection{Asymptotic considerations}
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em, itemsep=0.25em]
\item IGD: Maintaining $m$ threads with incremental posteriors yields per-decision overhead $O(m)$ for index evaluation and $O(1)$ for advancing the chosen thread, plus token generation cost for the LLM micro-action. With streaming updates to uncertainty proxies, the amortized update per thread remains $O(1)$, yielding overall $O(m + \text{tok})$ per scheduling step.
\item RS-MCTT: Per selection, UCT-style action choice is $O(|\mathcal{A}(s)|)$; rollouts cost $O(K \cdot L)$ tokens per expansion (with $K$ rollouts, average rollout length $L$). Backup along depth $D$ is $O(D)$ per iteration. Entropic risk evaluation adds $O(K)$ per backup node via a log-sum-exp; numerically stable computations are recommended.
\item ADR: Selecting the next probe by expected information gain per cost reduces to a one-step lookahead with conditional mutual information estimates. With $p$ candidate probes and a bounded-cost verifier, selection is $O(p)$ given cached entropy terms, and each probe execution costs $O(\text{tok}+c_{\text{ver}})$.
\item DRSM: Dual decomposition with price updates uses a few tens of iterations of gradient-like steps; each iteration evaluates subproblem utilities in parallel. Complexity is dominated by subproblem calls to the reasoner/verifier; the master price update is negligible.
\item BB-LLM: Branch-and-bound complexity is instance-dependent; admissible bounds ensure no loss in optimality when pruning. Overhead arises from bound computation and heuristic guidance, typically much cheaper than full expansions.
\item DEMI: External memory indexing uses approximate nearest-neighbor search; with episodic writes bounded per budget and a locality-sensitive hashing or HNSW index, retrieval is $O(\log M)$ with $M$ memory items; amortized refresh cost is sublinear.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Anytime behavior and stopping}
Under Proposition~\ref{prop:concavity}, marginal value-of-compute decreases with budget. EVC-based stopping that halts when the maximum positive EVC falls below a price $\lambda$ is consistent with a concave BPF. In RS-MCTT, risk-adjusted root values provide a principled stopping criterion balancing expected gains against compute costs; in IGD, lower-confidence indices provide conservative thresholds.

